#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""h713-panel -- the projector's settings page, served by the projector.

Until a remote control exists everything this projector can do is a command
line, and (Marco, 23.09.) one cannot keep doing all of it by command: so one
page, opened on a phone over the projector's own Wi-Fi or in a browser over
the LAN, with the vendor's "Projection Settings" and "Display settings" entries
in our own style.  NOTHING IS DECIDED HERE - every button is one existing
command of this project (h713-warp, h713-keystone, h713-focus, h713-autofocus,
h713-tv, h713-power, h713-remote-pair, h713-wifi, ...) or one `key = value` line
of a configuration file in /etc/h713, and this file only chooses which one and
reads the first `ok`/`error` line of the answer back to the page.  The browser
never names a command: it
names a verb out of COMMANDS, its arguments are checked against that table, and
the argument vector goes to subprocess without a shell, so a hole in the page
cannot become a hole in the projector.  Since 05.10.2026 the page asks for a
password (HTTP Basic Auth, any user name): it is generated at the first start,
kept in /etc/h713/panel.env (root, 0600) and read with --show-password.  Until
then whoever reached port 8080 could move the focus motor; on a LAN that was
everybody.

  h713-panel [--port 8080] [--address ADDR] [--tools DIR] [--page PATH]
             [--password-file PATH] [--no-auth] [--show-password] [--etc DIR]
  h713-panel --rollback-wifi [--etc DIR]     # what the Wi-Fi fall-back timer runs
"""

